# Shell scripts and SLURM batch files MUST be LF, always.
#
# This file exists because of a real failure. On Windows, git's autocrlf
# converted slurm/*.sh to CRLF in the working tree. Uploaded to the Linux
# cluster, `set -euo pipefail` became `pipefail\r`, and bash reported:
#
#     slurm/setup_env.sh: line 24: set: pipefail: invalid option name
#
# which names neither the line endings nor the file's origin. Every script
# would have failed the same way, in a different place each time.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.sbatch        text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf

# Python is run on both sides; LF everywhere keeps diffs clean and avoids
# the same class of surprise in any file with a shebang.
*.py            text eol=lf

# Data and docs: normalise on commit, let the platform decide on checkout.
*.yaml          text
*.yml           text
*.md            text
*.json          text
*.csv           text

# Binary: never touch.
*.npz           binary
*.zip           binary
*.png           binary
